### Anything else?

#### Cause

On the server, `<app-container>` does not render its slot, so the `<div>` and the projected text never reach the document. `serializeLView` records them as disconnected: in the `__nghData__` above, `"d":[1]` is the `<div>` in `WrapperComponent` and `"d":[3]` is the text in `App`.

On the client:

1. The text node in `App` is created, because it is disconnected. It is not inserted anywhere yet: its parent is the `<app-wrapper>` host, and the content of a component is inserted by that component's `ɵɵprojection`.
2. In `WrapperComponent`, `ɵɵelementStart` for the `<div>` calls `canHydrateNode`, which returns `false` for a disconnected node, so a new `<div>` is created.
3. `ɵɵprojection` for the `<ng-content />` inside that `<div>` skips `applyProjection`, because the view has hydration info.
4. When `open` becomes `true`, the `@if` view of `ContainerComponent` is created without hydration info. Its `ɵɵprojection` moves the `<div>` into `<app-container>`, and the `<div>` is still empty.

Step 3 is in [`packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts#L194-L204`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts#L194-L204) (`main` at `7391654`):

```ts
  const hydrationInfo = lView[HYDRATION];
  const isNodeCreationMode = !hydrationInfo || isInSkipHydrationBlock();
  const componentHostNode = lView[DECLARATION_COMPONENT_VIEW][T_HOST] as TElementNode;
  const isEmpty = componentHostNode.projection![tProjectionNode.projection] === null;

  if (isEmpty && fallbackIndex !== null) {
    insertFallbackContent(lView, tView, fallbackIndex);
  } else if (isNodeCreationMode && !isDetachedByI18n(tProjectionNode)) {
    // re-distribution of projectable nodes is stored on a component's view level
    applyProjection(tView, lView, tProjectionNode);
  }
```

Skipping `applyProjection` while hydrating is right when the slot's parent was hydrated, because the projected nodes are already in it. Here the parent was created on the client, so the projected nodes were created on the client too, and nothing inserts them.

The rows of the boundaries table that work match this reading:

- With `<ng-content />` directly in `<app-container>`, or inside an `<ng-container>`, step 4 inserts the text, because `applyProjectionRecursive` walks into nested `<ng-content>` slots and into the children of `<ng-container>`. It does not walk into the children of an element.
- With `<div><app-box><ng-content /></app-box></div>`, the host of `<app-box>` is created on the client, so its view has no hydration info and its own `ɵɵprojection` inserts the text.
- With `@if (true) { <div><ng-content /></div> }`, the `@if` view is created without hydration info, so its `ɵɵprojection` runs in creation mode.

#### Proposed fix

Treat the slot as being in creation mode when its parent was not hydrated. `canHydrateNode` ([`packages/core/src/hydration/utils.ts#L523-L532`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/core/src/hydration/utils.ts#L523-L532)) is the check that the element, `<ng-container>`, text and template instructions already use, through their `locateOrCreate…` functions, to decide whether to create a node. Applied to the slot's parent, it tells whether that parent was created on the client.

```diff
--- a/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts
+++ b/packages/core/src/render3/instructions/projection.ts
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  * Use of this source code is governed by an MIT-style license that can be
  * found in the LICENSE file at https://angular.dev/license
  */
+import {canHydrateNode} from '../../hydration/utils';
 import {findMatchingDehydratedView} from '../../hydration/views';
 import {isDetachedByI18n} from '../../i18n/utils';
 import {newArray} from '../../util/array_utils';
@@ -192,7 +193,15 @@
   setCurrentTNodeAsNotParent();

   const hydrationInfo = lView[HYDRATION];
-  const isNodeCreationMode = !hydrationInfo || isInSkipHydrationBlock();
+  const parentTNode = tProjectionNode.parent;
+  // While hydrating, the projected nodes are already in place in the DOM, so they are not
+  // inserted again. The exception is a slot whose parent was created on the client: it was
+  // disconnected from the DOM on the server (for example, inside content that was not
+  // projected), so the projected nodes were created on the client as well and must be inserted.
+  const isNodeCreationMode =
+    !hydrationInfo ||
+    isInSkipHydrationBlock() ||
+    (parentTNode !== null && !canHydrateNode(lView, parentTNode));
   const componentHostNode = lView[DECLARATION_COMPONENT_VIEW][T_HOST] as TElementNode;
   const isEmpty = componentHostNode.projection![tProjectionNode.projection] === null;
```

`text.ts`, `element.ts`, `element_container.ts` and `template.ts` in the same folder already import `canHydrateNode` from `../../hydration/utils`.

I applied the same condition to the compiled `@angular/core` 22.3.0 of the reproduction (`fesm2022/_debug_node-chunk.mjs`, in `ɵɵprojection`) and ran every row above, in development and production builds:

- Every row that lost the content now shows it: `<div>projected text</div>`, `<div><span>projected text</span></div>`, `<div><b>before</b>projected text</div>`, `<div><b>projected element</b></div>`, `<div><b>b</b> text</div>`, `<app-box><div>projected text</div></app-box>`. It hides on the second click and shows again on the third.
- The rows that already worked give the same content as without the change, including `open` set to `true` on the server, where the server-rendered `<div>` is reused.
- The server output does not change: the prerendered HTML and `__nghData__` are identical with and without the change. The change only affects the client, since `withDomHydration` does not enable the hydration runtime when `ngServerMode` is set, so `lView[HYDRATION]` is `null` on the server.
- Hydration does not change: the server-rendered `<button>`, `<app-wrapper>` and `<app-container>` are reused, the development build logs the same `Angular hydrated N component(s) and M node(s)` counts as without the change, and there is no NG05xx error or any other console output.

It also does not interact with #72129. With and without the one-line fix proposed there, in development and production builds, the rows of the #72129 table give the results below. The fix in #72131 narrows that condition to content-projected nodes; every node that follows a slot in these rows is projected into `<app-container>`, so the condition matches the same nodes there.

| Template inside `<app-container [open]="open()">` | `open` on the server | Unpatched | #72129 fix | This fix | Both |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>after</p>` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>after</p>` |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `true` | `projected text<p>after</p>` | same | same | same |
| `<ng-content />` | `false` | `projected text` | same | same | same |
| `<ng-content /><p>after</p>` | `false` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>after</p>` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>after</p>` |
| `<ng-content />after` | `false` | NG0502 | `projected textafter` | NG0502 | `projected textafter` |
| `<ng-content /><ng-container>after</ng-container>` | `false` | NG0502 | `projected textafter` | NG0502 | `projected textafter` |
| `<ng-content />@for (i of [1]; track i) { <p>{{ i }}</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>1</p>` | NG0502 | `projected text<p>1</p>` |
| `<div><ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }</div>` | `false` | NG0502 | `<div><p>after</p></div>` | NG0502 | `<div>projected text<p>after</p></div>` |
| `<p>before</p><ng-content />` | `false` | `<p>before</p>projected text` | same | same | same |
| `<ng-content select="b" /><ng-content />` | `false` | `projected text` | same | same | same |
| `@let x = 1;@if (true) { <p>{{ x }}</p> }` (no `<ng-content>`) | `false` | NG0502 | `<p>1</p>` | NG0502 | `<p>1</p>` |
| `<ng-content />@let x = 1;` | `false` | `projected text` | same | same | same |

The `<div>` row needs both fixes: the #72129 fix lets it render on the server, and this fix restores the projected text in the `<div>`.

A narrower alternative only checks the disconnected-node list: `parentTNode !== null && isDisconnectedNode(hydrationInfo, parentTNode.index - HEADER_OFFSET)`. It gave the same results on every row above. `canHydrateNode` also covers a parent detached by i18n, which I did not test.

I have not run Angular's own test suite with the change.

#### Suggested regression test

In `packages/platform-server/test/full_app_hydration_spec.ts`, in `describe('partial projection')`, next to "should support cases when some element nodes are not projected (@if)" (currently [line 5393](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/73916540c1688c0463617ef1469bf63adb0a628f/packages/platform-server/test/full_app_hydration_spec.ts#L5393)). It uses only helpers and symbols the spec already imports (`Input`, `signal`). I have not run it in the Angular repository.

```ts
it('should project content into an element that was not projected on the server', async () => {
  @Component({
    selector: 'app-container',
    template: `
      @if (open) {
        <ng-content />
      }
    `,
  })
  class ContainerComponent {
    @Input() open = false;
  }

  @Component({
    selector: 'app-wrapper',
    imports: [ContainerComponent],
    template: `
      <app-container [open]="open">
        <div><ng-content /></div>
      </app-container>
    `,
  })
  class WrapperComponent {
    @Input() open = false;
  }

  @Component({
    selector: 'app',
    imports: [WrapperComponent],
    template: `<app-wrapper [open]="open()">projected text</app-wrapper>`,
  })
  class SimpleComponent {
    open = signal(false);
  }

  const html = await ssr(SimpleComponent);
  const ssrContents = getAppContents(html);

  expect(ssrContents).toContain('<app ngh');
  expect(ssrContents).not.toContain('projected text');

  resetTViewsFor(SimpleComponent, WrapperComponent, ContainerComponent);

  const appRef = await prepareEnvironmentAndHydrate(doc, html, SimpleComponent);
  const compRef = getComponentRef<SimpleComponent>(appRef);
  appRef.tick();

  const clientRootNode = compRef.location.nativeElement;
  verifyAllNodesClaimedForHydration(clientRootNode);
  verifyClientAndSSRContentsMatch(ssrContents, clientRootNode);

  // The content that was not projected on the server renders once the slot opens.
  compRef.instance.open.set(true);
  appRef.tick();

  expect(clientRootNode.querySelector('app-container > div').textContent).toBe('projected text');
});
```

Without the fix, I expect the last expectation to fail with an empty `<div>`, as in the reproduction.

#### Related

- #72129: same family (nodes in content that is not projected), but a different mechanism. That bug fails on the server, in `serializeLView`, when a node follows `<ng-content>`. This one happens on the client, in `ɵɵprojection`, and needs no sibling. The two fixes are independent. #72131 is the pull request for #72129.
- Possibly related: #50175 reported the same symptom in 2023 ("the ng-content is filled with nothing", with Angular Material). According to the analysis in that issue, its cause was a view container projected into a component that skips hydration, and it was fixed in 16.0.1.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

It reproduces on 20.3.35, 21.2.26, 22.2.1, 22.3.0-rc.0 (the current `next` tag) and 22.3.0 (the current `latest`).

### Description

With hydration enabled, server rendering fails with `NG0502: During serialization, Angular was unable to find an element in the DOM` when all of these hold:

1. A component template (`WrapperComponent`) places `<ng-content />` inside the content of another component (`<app-container>`).
2. In that content, a node follows the `<ng-content />` as a sibling: a control-flow block, an element, a text node, an `<ng-container>`, or a `@for`.
3. The other component (`ContainerComponent`) does not render its own `<ng-content>` on the server, for example because the slot sits inside an `@if` that is false.

Content that is not projected is a supported case. Hydration marks such nodes as disconnected (`appendDisconnectedNodeIndex`), and the existing test "should support cases when some element nodes are not projected (@if)" covers it. The failure only happens when a sibling follows the `<ng-content />`.

The same failure happens with no `<ng-content>` at all when a `@let` declaration is followed by a sibling in content that is not projected. `@let` and `<ng-content>` share the same branch of `serializeLView`.

Boundaries (`ng build`, which prerenders `/`):

| Template inside `<app-container [open]="open()">` | `open` on the server | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }` | `true` | OK |
| `<ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `<ng-content /><p>after</p>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />after` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content /><ng-container>after</ng-container>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@for (i of [1]; track i) { <p>{{ i }}</p> }` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<div><ng-content />@if (true) { <p>after</p> }</div>` | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<p>before</p><ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `<ng-content select="b" /><ng-content />` | `false` | OK |
| `@let x = 1;@if (true) { <p>{{ x }}</p> }` (no `<ng-content>`) | `false` | NG0502 |
| `<ng-content />@let x = 1;` | `false` | OK |

The bug does not depend on what is projected into `<app-wrapper>`: it also fails when nothing is projected.

With `RenderMode.Server` instead of prerendering (and `localhost` in `security.allowedHosts`), the request answers HTTP 200 with the body `Internal server error.` and nothing is logged, because the error happens while the response streams.

