Hi Angular team 👋

I ran into a small surprise with `keyvalue: null`. I reversed the entries in a `Map` and passed the new map to my template. A plain `@for` picked up the new order, but the row using the pipe stayed in the old order.

### How to reproduce

Paste this into `src/main.ts` in an Angular CLI app, run `ng serve`, and click **Reverse** once.

```ts
import {KeyValuePipe} from '@angular/common';
import {Component, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [KeyValuePipe],
  template: `
    <p>Direct: @for (pair of rows(); track pair[0]) { <span>{{ pair[0] }}</span> }</p>
    <p>Pipe: @for (pair of rows() | keyvalue: null; track pair.key) { <span>{{ pair.key }}</span> }</p>
    <button (click)="reverse()">Reverse</button>
  `,
})
class App {
  readonly rows = signal(new Map([['b', 2], ['a', 1], ['c', 3]]));

  reverse() {
    this.rows.update(rows => new Map([...rows].reverse()));
  }
}

bootstrapApplication(App);
```

| | Before the click | After the click |
|---|---|---|
| Direct iteration | `b a c` | `c a b` |
| `keyvalue: null` | `b a c` | `b a c` |

I expected both rows to show `c a b`, since passing `null` tells the pipe to use the input’s natural order. If I then change the value of just one entry, the pipe catches up. [KeyValuePipe docs](https://angular.dev/api/common/KeyValuePipe#description)

I also tried calling `transform()` directly. A fresh pipe gives me `c a b` for the reversed map. A pipe that already processed the original map gives me `b a c` for that same reversed map.

I found #71893 while looking into this. That example switches the comparator to `null`. Here it’s `null` from the first render, and only the insertion order changes.

